						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1208.msg12859#msg12859"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1208.msg12859#msg12859">Re: new car, dealerships mark up?</a></b><br />
									<span class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #12 on:</b> November 28, 2004, 11:00:45 am &#187;</span></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							Great post/info Wyztyz, alot of that stuff I didn&#039;t know about.&nbsp; I found a dealership who is selling at no more than MSRP, but it will be quite awhile before they get what I want without ordering it.&nbsp; Orders are taking around 4 months.<br /><br />How do you go about talking with the fleet sales people?&nbsp; I&#039;ve been told that it&#039;s much easier to get a better deal with them.<br /><br />Also I have a good credit score, 750ish.&nbsp; Any idea what kind of 48 month APR I would have a chance at negotiating?&nbsp; I saw fords &quot;incentive&quot; financing plans and looks like 5-6% @ 48 months for the new mustang.&nbsp; I don&#039;t want to ding my credit rating by applying for credit just to turn it down due to an unfair APR.&nbsp; Think they would be able to quote me a theoretical APR if my crdit turns out to be as good as I say it is?<br /><br />Lastly, would I be better off ordering a car specific to what I want or buying one off the lot, will I be able to negotiate a better deal by ordering it?<br /><br />I&#039;ve already resigned myself to wait until lots start holding some inventory, as is, they (atleast the GT&#039;s) are selling as they come off the truck.&nbsp; The last dealership I went to told me they held on to a V6 model for over a week, which was a total anomoly.
						</td>
					</tr>
