								<br />
								<img src="http://forums.gucomics.com/images/avatars/20002703583fbef1af3a4d3.gif" width="65" height="65" alt="" border="0" /><br />
								<a href="http://web.icq.com/whitepages/about_me/1,,,00.html?Uin=265105278" target="_blank"><img src="http://web.icq.com/whitepages/online?icq=265105278&amp;img=5" alt="265105278" width="18" height="18" border="0" /></a>
								<a href="http://members.msn.com/Wyztyz" target="_blank"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/msntalk.gif" alt="Wyztyz" border="0" /></a>
								<a href="http://edit.yahoo.com/config/send_webmesg?.target=Wyztyz"><img src="http://opi.yahoo.com/online?u=Wyztyz&amp;m=g&amp;t=0" alt="Wyztyz" border="0" /></a>
								<a href="aim:goim?screenname=Wyztyz&amp;message=Hi.+Are+you+there?"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/aim.gif" alt="Wyztyz" border="0" /></a><br />
								<a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=167"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/icons/profile_sm.gif" alt="View Profile" title="View Profile" border="0" /></a>
								<a href="http://www.dragonsfist.org" title="Dragons Fist" target="_blank"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/www_sm.gif" alt="WWW" border="0" /></a>
								<a href="mailto:wyztyz@aol.com"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/email_sm.gif" alt="Email" title="Email" border="0" /></a>
							</span>
						</td>
						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1208.msg12876#msg12876"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1208.msg12876#msg12876">Re: new car, dealerships mark up?</a></b><br />
									<span class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #13 on:</b> November 29, 2004, 05:25:35 pm &#187;</span></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							Anyone who has a credit score of higher than 730 and a five year &quot;applied for&quot; credit history can pretty much get the lowest going rates.  Authorized user accounts don&#039;t count as &quot;applied for&quot; credit.  You can go to <a href="http://www.bankrate.com" target="_blank">http://www.bankrate.com</a> to check most recent rates on auto loans, mortgages, credit cards, etc.  <br /><br />As long as you have the tools to make an educated choice, you really don&#039;t have to pit dealer against another dealer for pricing. Often times Sales Personnel will say anything over the phone to get someone in the door to negotiate with them.  It&#039;s easier to negotiate with a customer who is actually there at the dealership than on the phone.  The Sales People know this.  It really wastes the consumers time.  How many times did a Sales Person promise a deal over the phone and when you went to that dealer, you haggled anyway?  <br /><br />1) If you are serious, Just Go in.  (Most Sales People are commission only.  Try not to waste your time or theirs.  They have families to feed too.) <br />2) Ask to speak with the Actual Sales Manager or Fleet Manager.  Let them know that you want to talk numbers with only them.  (Do not negtiate with the Sales Person or Asst. Sales Manager also known as a Closer.  If they have to check to talk to anyone about prices then they are most likely not the Actual Sales Manager.)<br />3) Pick out the vehicle that you like and take a test drive with the Sales Person or Fleet Manager.  (The Sales Manager will more than likely send you on a test drive with someone who is more knowledgeable about the vehicle than a Newbie Sales Person.)  <br />4) Ask the Sales Manager to see the copy of the Invoice on the vehicle that you choose.  <br />5) Make an offer that you think is fair over the invoice.  (If its a reasonable offer over invoice, any dealer will take it depending on the demand for the vehicle.)<br />6) Keep the Car Sale in your local area so the sales tax dollars will be spent on your community.  (A few hundred dollars is not worth thousands that can be lost to benefit the community you live in.) 
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
